{
  "_notes": "World layout (A1, re-laid-out in A2 and before A3). Positions are floor tiles: x runs down-right on screen, y runs down-left; [x, y, w, h] rectangles. 'building' is the garage (concrete floor, back walls on its x = 0 and y = 0 edges); 'walk' is where people can walk; 'areas' are ground colors drawn under everything. A station's 'at' is what it stands on (blocks walking once built), 'mat' is where you stand to use it; while a station is locked its price pad sits on its mat. unlock.priceFrom: 'drone' = that pad's drone price, 'hire' = the role's hirePrice (workers.json). rigUpgrade pads sell the next PC tier (pc_tiers.json) for their target rig. Counter: customers line up at 'queue' spots (front first; extra customers continue the line), walk in from 'enter', leave along 'exit'. Launch pad 'loop' = centre x, y and radius of the drone's flight loop. Art lives under assets/images/world/. Tall stations hide what's up-screen of them, so they stand against the back walls; test/layout_test.dart checks in screen space that no pad is under any station's art or the customer line, and that every name tag is next to its own station. Kiosks print one ticket every everySec divided by the number of launch pads in their zone (more pads, more tickets) and hold 'stack' tickets. Menu stations (office, mirror, miner, workbench, Help Wanted) open after standing still on them for menuHoldSec; pickup/drop-off mats and price pads work right away. A4 zones (tool/gen_zones.py writes them from one template): each zone has a 'gate' station (a zoneGate price pad standing in the zone before it, with a rep requirement); until it's bought the zone can't be entered and its stations don't exist. Stations without a price pad in a zone are built when the zone opens (the gate price covers its first drone and stations). Kiosk 'jobs' limits which jobs it prints. Rig 'tier' is the PC it starts with. unlock.license: also needs that license (Part 137 for the spray pad). Launch pad drone 'second' = the garage drone you didn't start with.",
